\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The model's transition conserves the integrator's shadow Hamiltonian, not textbook energy. Both panels sweep the target $T_c = E + c\,\dot  \theta \qopname  \relax o{sin}\theta $; the dotted line is $c^\star = 0.125$ predicted from the integrator with no fitting. \textbf  {Left:} on ground-truth trajectories, with no model involved, $T_{c^\star }$ is $591\times $ better conserved than textbook energy. \textbf  {Right:} the learned transition agrees, on all three seeds. The sweep passes from the supervised probe's level (red) down to the label-free scalar's (blue) exactly at $c^\star $. The wrong-sign control at $c=-c^\star $ is $12\times $ worse (median over seeds), so this is not monotone in $|c|$.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:shadow}{{2}{7}{The model's transition conserves the integrator's shadow Hamiltonian, not textbook energy. Both panels sweep the target $T_c = E + c\,\dot \theta \sin \theta $; the dotted line is $c^\star = 0.125$ predicted from the integrator with no fitting. \textbf {Left:} on ground-truth trajectories, with no model involved, $T_{c^\star }$ is $591\times $ better conserved than textbook energy. \textbf {Right:} the learned transition agrees, on all three seeds. The sweep passes from the supervised probe's level (red) down to the label-free scalar's (blue) exactly at $c^\star $. The wrong-sign control at $c=-c^\star $ is $12\times $ worse (median over seeds), so this is not monotone in $|c|$.\relax }{figure.caption.23}{}}
